\section{Read Arcs as Operand Protocols}

In a token dataflow graph, nodes denote operations and directed arcs carry values. A node is fireable when the tokens required by one of its firing rules are available. Firing consumes the specified inputs, performs the operation, and produces specified outputs. Readiness is not completion: a runtime may delay a ready firing, and several ready nodes may execute concurrently. The pure model has no program counter selecting a unique next node \cite{johnston2004dataflow}.

An arc is more than a drawn dependence. Its protocol says whether it is a one-place operand slot, a FIFO stream, or part of a tagged matching discipline. Forking must duplicate a token when two consumers both need the value. An uncontrolled merge is not equivalent to a deterministic conditional merge because arrival order can become observable.

\section{Fire a Small Arithmetic Graph}

Use this textual graph notation, where each input port consumes one token and each output port produces one:

\begin{verbatim}
input x -> fork fx
const  2 -> mul.left
fx.a     -> mul.right
fx.b     -> add.left
mul.out  -> add.right
add.out  -> output y
\end{verbatim}

For input token \(3\), \texttt{fork} creates two \(3\) tokens. Then \texttt{mul} produces \(6\), while \texttt{add} must wait for both \(3\) and \(6\), finally producing \(9\). The firing order between the fork's output deliveries and other independent firings is implementation latitude; consuming an operand twice is not. \Cref{fig:visual-353} depicts the token obligations.

