\section{Implement Firing Without Inventing Semantics}

\begin{verbatim}
enqueue(input_x, 3)
while output_y is empty:
    n := choose_any_ready_node()
    args := atomically_consume(n.firing_rule.inputs)
    results := evaluate_pure_operator(n.op, args)
    append_in_port_order(n.outputs, results)
return dequeue(output_y)
\end{verbatim}

Atomic consumption prevents two firings from claiming the same token. Fair selection can matter for liveness, but does not license firing without operands. The example has a unique finite result under every schedule that eventually executes ready nodes.

\section{Represent and Rewrite the Graph}

A suitable IR has operator, fork, merge, input, and output nodes; typed, port-numbered FIFO edges; firing rules and production/consumption rates; explicit delay or feedback state; optional clocks or tags; effect summaries; and source provenance on nodes and synthetic forks. Compilation may emit a graph interpreter table, a distributed actor network, scheduled sequential code, or hardware with handshake storage.

Legal optimizations include folding \(\texttt{mul}(x,2)\) if numeric overflow and exceptions are preserved, and node fusion when token order, rates, and firing atomicity are unchanged. Semantic-preservation risks include removing a fork, coalescing two queues, or speculating an operator: these can lose tokens, change blocking, or expose a trap. A validator should compare output token sequences and externally visible effects, and should retain provenance so a changed firing rule can be traced back to the source construct that required it.

\section{Trace the Join in Port Order}

Give the multiplier's constant port a per-firing literal operand, not a FIFO preloaded with one copy of \(2\). With a FIFO literal, the first firing would consume the only token, and the graph would stop producing results on the next input. The fork, in contrast, produces one token on each of two queues for every input it consumes. For the input \(3\) the queue state evolves as follows; the literal is not listed because it is not buffered.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\hline
After firing & Into multiplier & Into add, left & Into add, right \\
\hline
Fork & \(3\) & \(3\) & empty \\
Multiply & empty & \(3\) & \(6\) \\
Add & empty & empty & empty \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The add can fire only in the third row's transition, after both of its operands are present. The arithmetic result is \(9\). Replace addition with subtraction temporarily: the expected result is then \(-3\), which detects an implementation that assembles operands in arrival order instead of named port order. Even with pure operators, the two operands are not interchangeable.

\begin{verbatim}
try_fire(node):
    if any required FIFO prefix is missing: return BLOCKED
    operands := []
    for port in declared_operand_order(node):
        if port is literal: append(operands, port.literal)
        else: append(operands, peek(port.queue))
    results := run_pure_total_operator(node, operands)
    if outputs cannot all be committed: return BLOCKED
    for port in consumed_ports(node): pop(port.queue)
    for port in output_ports(node): push(port.queue, results[port])
    return FIRED
\end{verbatim}

This sketch assumes a single scheduler and total operators. Under concurrent scheduling, reserve output space and make the readiness check and commit one transaction. Reserving only one output of a fork may let a consumer advance on one branch while the producer retries the entire firing and publishes a duplicate. Test two input items and stall the consumer of one branch. A sound finite implementation either publishes both copies or leaves the input untouched at that firing boundary.

\paragraph{Check a full queue at resume.}
Record each fork's logical firing number on both outputs in debug builds.
At the join, assert that the numbers at the two heads agree. This is redundant
for correctly implemented one-to-one FIFO branches but makes a dropped
or duplicated emission show up at the first mismatched join instead of
as a mysterious wrong result downstream.
